-- TRIEL example inside the formal core: the notification of a personal data
-- breach to the supervisory authority, after Article 33 of the GDPR, as an
-- obligation with a deadline and a cure (ON_BREACH ... CURE_BY).
--
-- This is a stylised reading of the article, not a formalisation of it.
-- Article 33(1) requires the controller to notify the authority without undue
-- delay and, where feasible, not later than 72 hours after having become aware
-- of the breach; where the notification is not made within 72 hours, it shall
-- be accompanied by reasons for the delay. The example reads this as a
-- deadline of 72 hours whose lapse is a breach, which a later notification
-- cures but does not undo.
--
-- Durations count from the activation of the specification
-- (formal/core/CORE.md, section 8.3, point 5). Here the activation is the
-- moment when the controller became aware of the breach, so the controller
-- must notify the authority within 72 hours of it; a notification exactly at
-- the deadline fulfils (fulfilled_at_deadline).
--
-- Once the trace passes the deadline without a notification, the obligation
-- is breached at the deadline, and the breach stays on record. The handler
-- opens a cure window of 7 days from the moment of breach (section 8.1,
-- "Status after a breach"): a notification in that window cures the breach,
-- which is then reported as BREACHED ... CURE FULFILLED (cure_restores); a
-- cure window that passes without one is a second breach, and it is final
-- (cure_failed, section 8.3, point 9). The cure window is (d, d + 7 days]:
-- only a notification strictly after the deadline cures, and one at or before
-- the deadline fulfils the obligation itself, so nothing is cured.
--
-- The 7 days are an assumption of this example: Article 33 sets no period for
-- a late notification, and the core needs a duration in CURE_BY. The cure is
-- the same action as the obligation, notify(authority): the core does not
-- distinguish a late notification with reasons from one without. The reasons
-- are recorded in the factor delay_reasons, which no verdict reads; that a
-- late notification must be accompanied by them is a condition on its
-- content, outside this example.
--
-- Article 33(1) also exempts a breach that is unlikely to result in a risk to
-- the rights and freedoms of natural persons, and Article 33(4) allows the
-- information to be provided in phases. The exemption needs a conditional
-- term whose branch has a deadline, which is outside the core: the translator
-- rejects a deadline in a composite term, the branches of IF and WHEN
-- included (formal/core/CORE.md, section 10.6, further rejections). Neither
-- is part of this example.
--
-- The SUBJECTS block declares the controller, the subject of the terms
-- (formal/core/CORE.md, section 9, decision 29), the authority, which an
-- action argument may name only if it is declared (section 10.6, rule 9),
-- and the data subjects, whom Article 34 may require the controller to
-- inform: they are declared so that a scenario can show that a communication
-- to them is not the notification to the authority. No factor comes from an
-- ORACLE and there is no PENALTY, so neither PROVENANCE_REQUIRED nor CURRENCY
-- is required (decision 28). Every construct in this file is translated into
-- the Isabelle/HOL core (formal/core) and evaluated by the exported evaluator
-- (evaluator/).

SPECIFICATION gdpr_art33_notification VERSION 1.0.0
    JURISDICTION "EU"

SUBJECTS {
  controller    : PARTY,
  authority     : REGULATOR,
  data_subjects : PARTY
}

TERMS {
  controller MUST notify(authority) WITHIN 72 HOURS;
  controller ON_BREACH CURE_BY 7 DAYS
}

FACTORS {
  affected_records : Integer METADATA SOURCE controller,
  delay_reasons    : Optional<String> METADATA SOURCE controller
}

INVARIANTS {
  records_known : LIVENESS : EVENTUALLY(PRESENT(affected_records))
}
